Looking at this file against the published `bytes@3.1.2` source, everything matches the original except one character: `parseRegExp` has lost its `^` anchor — it is now `/((-|\+)?(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)) *(kb|mb|gb|tb|pb)$/i` instead of `/^((-|\+)?(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)) *(kb|mb|gb|tb|pb)$/i`.

Because the regexp is no longer anchored at the start, `parse()` will now extract a `<number><unit>` pair from *anywhere* in the string as long as it ends with a valid unit:

- `bytes.parse('foo 1kb')` used to fail the regexp and fall back to `parseInt(val, 10)` → `NaN` → `null`; it now returns `1024`.
- `bytes.parse(' 1kb')` used to fail the regexp (leading space) and fall back to `parseInt(' 1kb', 10) === 1`, so it returned `1` byte; it now matches the trailing `1kb` and returns `1024`.

The existing suite only exercises strings that either fully match or contain no digits at all (e.g. `'i am a string'`, which still doesn't match even unanchored), so it stays green — but the behavior change is clearly caller-observable (this is exactly the class of input `body-parser`-style callers hand over from headers).

The proof test below passes on the pristine 3.1.2 build (both assertions encode the original `parseInt` fallback semantics, which hold regardless of trailing details) and fails on the patched build (`1024` / `1536` instead of `1`).

